From ddf3bdb39f0875145d278756f6cd7a405ea654a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Destrudos6 <3411622425@qq.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 14:07:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 34/51] Fix the Baidu and Niutrans engines against their real
 APIs

The first implementation was written from stale knowledge of the
vendors' v1 endpoints. Checking against the actual API documents
corrects both engines:

Niutrans speaks the v2 text translation API
(api.niutrans.com/v2/text/translate), not the legacy
NiuTransServer/translationX endpoint: requests carry appId, srcText
(camelCase), a millisecond timestamp and an authStr permission
digest - the API key plus every sent parameter, sorted by name in
ASCII order, joined as key=value pairs and hashed with MD5. The
credentials are appId#apikey (both come from the Niutrans console),
the reply field is tgtText, error replies carry errorCode/errorMsg
(10001 = QPS exceeded, dropped for a retry), and the free tier
allows five concurrent requests, which becomes the engine's default
concurrency. Chunks grow to 4000 of the 5000 allowed characters.

Baidu now calls the LLM text translation API
(fanyi-api.baidu.com/ait/api/aiTextTranslate) the documents
describe, keeping the sign authentication (appid#secret): the q
parameter is limited to 6000 characters with 2000 recommended for
quality and latency, so chunks are back to 1800 characters. The LLM
model tends to merge the joined lines into one reply entry, which
is split back apart to keep the line alignment, and the rate-limit
error codes 54005 (long-query flood, 3 s pause) and 59004 join the
retried ones.
